Use os.read() instead of BufferedReader for the exabgp process API

ExaBGP's reactor reads helper-process output with
proc.stdout.read(16384). proc.stdout is a BufferedReader whose internal
buffer (io.DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE, 8192) can hold bytes that select.poll()
cannot see, because poll() watches the kernel fd. When a partial read
returns between 8193 and 16383 bytes, CPython fills the internal buffer
and keeps the surplus there. On the next reactor cycle poll() reports no
data and the process is skipped.

Mid-batch this is harmless because later writes re-trigger poll(), but at
the tail of a batch the writer has finished, nothing re-triggers poll()
and the buffered commands are lost. The HTTP API still returns 200, so the
loss is silent: a large "announce route ...;withdraw route ..." batch is
only partially applied.

os.read() reads the kernel fd directly and never consumes more than it
returns, so poll() and the reader always agree.

Upstream issue:  https://github.com/Exa-Networks/exabgp/issues/1421
Upstream fix:    https://github.com/Exa-Networks/exabgp/pull/1422
SONiC tracking:  https://github.com/sonic-net/sonic-mgmt/issues/28034

The upstream fix was merged to the exabgp 5.0 branch on 2026-09-06, after
the latest release (5.0.13, 2026-08-24). No released version carries it, so
it is applied here as a patch.

When bumping exabgp, note how the Dockerfile step behaves:

  * If the new version already contains the fix, the step detects the
    patched call, skips this patch and the build succeeds. Delete this file
    and the step at that point.

  * If the new version still has the defect, this patch will NOT apply and
    the build fails on purpose. 5.0 spells the same line differently
    (buf = str(proc.stdout.read(16384), 'ascii') instead of
    buf = str_ascii(proc.stdout.read(16384))), so the patch has to be
    refreshed against the new source rather than force-applied.

  * Upstream has moved the active reader to received_async() on the
    development branch, leaving the sync received() as dead code. If a
    future version ships both, patching received() alone is not enough --
    check which one the reactor actually drives before refreshing.

--- a/exabgp/reactor/api/processes.py
+++ b/exabgp/reactor/api/processes.py
@@ -232,7 +232,7 @@
                     # Calling next() on Linux and OSX works perfectly well
                     # but not on OpenBSD where it always raise StopIteration
                     # and only read() works (not even readline)
-                    buf = str_ascii(proc.stdout.read(16384))
+                    buf = str_ascii(os.read(proc.stdout.fileno(), 16384))
                     if buf == '' and poll is not None:
                         # if proc.poll() is None then
                         # process is fine, we received an empty line because
